\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Instead of answering your question, I refer you to the evidence of the text. That is, you can answer your own question, though I can offer places in the Bible you might look to do so.\
Looking together then at the evidence, Ezekiel 23 does indeed locate the corruption even back to before rescue. Ezekiel 16 hinted at this, calling the parentage of Jerusalem Canaanite. But this is not so much a theological problem as an invitation to reread the narrative of Israel in Egypt, and the discussion of that time elsewhere. Consider the reaction of the people to Moses in Exodus 5. Or the look backwards to that time in Joshua 24:14, implying that the people served other gods in Egypt. Or the implications in Deuteronomy that the LORD's choice to rescue the previous generation had not been because of anything good about that generation, but because of promises to their ancestors. Moreover, Deuteronomy 9:7 says that the rescued generation provoked the LORD from the day they came out of the land of Egypt; such wickedness can hardly have been caused by the rescue. No - that same chapter of Deuteronomy says about giving the people the promised land that this was not due to the righteousness of the people - a different generation there, but the same assumption that of course we are good, of course we deserve good things.\
Chapter 23 of Ezekiel has similar rhetorical devastation. Like the people to whom Moses spoke, or Ezekiel, the people to whom the Bible speaks today may need reminders that however much they see themselves and people like themselves as basically good, the LORD sees them as they are.\
Perhaps to that end verse 19 suggests active recollection and return to pattern, as you say. But a recollection of what, a pattern of what? The intervening verses express a pattern that goes beyond financial motives for sexual activity: lust. Inside the metaphor, if this is a pattern, it is one of being consumed by inappropriate desire, and allowing that desire to conceive and birth sexual sin to a shocking extent.\
What, beyond the metaphor of sisterhood, the names of the sisters contribute to this metaphor, I do not know, though I suspect rhythm and sound are as likely to play a role as your theory. But without a connection to the larger rhetoric of the chapter, such theories do not help us much.\
And yes, even considering the shock of verse 19 when you think it through, verse 20 still hits with extraordinary force. The more so when you think what part of the flesh this is talking about. \
Both the memory and the flesh are interpreted by verse 21. Whether the verb of verse 21 is looking backwards, parallel with memory, or looking forwards, parallel with the craving of verse 20, the overall idea is clear. \
This is like Jeremiah 2:23-24, where the people are compared to a donkey in heat - no one has to try very hard to have sex with her, due to her lust. The behavior in an animal is understandable. But the extreme language here, while it shocks when you remember who it is talking about, may perhaps evoke disgust at such internal abandon and the consequent external debasement. If that disgust can be transferred to the conduct of the people, then the shock may serve to drive that transfer deeper into memory or past the defenses like 'of course we are good people, of course we are a good nation'.\
The more so when you consider the heart attitude and conduct for which that lust and whoreish behavior is a metaphor.\
But we must be careful here. What you say about verse 39 is true. And how you use it to interpret verse 20 I think is true. But, precisely because of how important these ideas are, we need to be careful to put them on a proper foundation of evidence, establishing that all the evidence in the chapter is consistent with those theories, or else finding inconsistencies. \
To get there, we would need to consider the structure of this chapter. We have the introduction of the daughters, then the history of the elder daughter's behavior and its outcome, then the history of the younger daughter's behavior, then the declaration of the sentence for that behavior spoken to the younger daughter, and only then formal judgment with its charges and its punishment. We may need to refine or even replace this structure if we find that the evidence points to a different structure. But we can start with this.\
But also, to take the warning from Ezekiel 16 seriously, we need to take seriously the claim the text opens with, that this is the word of the LORD. At a minimum, that means treating each word of this chapter as part of that message. To that end, I throw back to you a more specific version of your question: what does the first section demand of us?
