\f1  ("and Egypt you will not remember anymore").\
The parallel structure suggests these are equivalent outcomes. Knowing YHWH means no longer remembering Egypt. The recognition of who YHWH is specifically involves the end of that compulsive remembering, that longing for Egypt that verse 19 described.\
This connects back to the pattern established from the beginning. The prostitution started in Egypt (v. 3). She kept returning to it mentally (v. 19 - "remembering the days of her youth"). The addiction was rooted in this idealized memory of Egypt as source of provision, security, power.\
So the judgment that ends the lewdness also ends the Egypt-memory. The two are inseparable. To truly know YHWH - operationally, not just theoretically - requires that Egypt stop being the reference point, the object of desire, the remembered source of good.\
The disgust the audience should feel at the sexual addiction pattern is meant to make them recognize their own Egypt-longing as equally pathological. And when judgment breaks that pattern, that itself is how they'll know YHWH - not through abstract theological recognition, but through the concrete end of the compulsion.
