\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Part of the problem is that the original audience has one last element of distance - they came from Jerusalem, but they are not the current inhabitants of Jerusalem. They are guilty of many of the same things, but the judgement will not fall on them.\
But they still value the good name of Jerusalem, having been its elite and hoping to return. Thus, the many references to that reputation being destroyed utterly. Here, the prostitution metaphor sets up the reputation destruction. The adulteress is used as a prostitute. What she prepares carefully for as high status seduction leads to low status use as a low status sex object, and then public execution as a murderer and adulteress. She is either so wicked or so poorly off that in verse 43, the prophet cannot even finish the explanation of just how bad it is.\
Part of the problem is the degree of compartmentalization that allows everything from Sabbath profanation to child sacrifice to operate unhindered by the word of the LORD. The text does, as you say, remind them that they and the inhabitants of Jerusalem are violating specific covenant, that what they give away was given in that covenant, that they are compartmentalizing, that they are liable to judgement under that covenant. But that is not enough. The text calls the Babylonian army executing sentence 'righteous men', and the sisters, adulteresses and blood shedders and lewd women. The Babylonian army naturally would fall under military and political spheres, and if the moral sphere was considered at all in that context, righteous would hardly be the adjective used. But the LORD describes what will happen in terms of his own sentence on the conduct of the sisters, for it is against the LORD who rules all nations, all armies, all history that the sisters sinned.\
Now, you've seen enough of my theory of the text, if in bits and pieces of comment on what you said or lack thereof and in calling attention to different elements of the evidence. But before we get to the final warning, it is important not only to put everything together into a coherent theory and flow of the rhetoric of the oracle. That theory needs to not only account for all the evidence in the text in a way that seems good from the outside, it needs to properly hear the demand of the text - or else we will hardly understand the warning, much less heed it. Moreover, as when one writes up a proof, we may find that we have not properly accounted for all the evidence, or that our theory is less coherent than we thought.\
