\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Reading the Ezekiel 22 message alongside Ezekiel 23 reveals something beyond simple recasting:\
Ezekiel 22's warning could be understood as: "When bloodshed appears - whether as violence, economic exploitation, or systemic contempt for life - recognize it and stand in the breach." That's about discernment in the moment of decision.\
But Ezekiel 23 diagnoses the 
\i structural addiction
\i0  that prevents that discernment from ever happening.\
The sisters didn't fail to recognize that child sacrifice was wrong. They sacrificed children in the morning and entered the sanctuary the same evening (v. 39). They compartmentalized completely. The domains were separated: "this space for securing favor from these powers, that space for maintaining covenant relationship."\
This means the bloodshed invisibility isn't primarily an epistemic problem ("didn't notice") but an addiction problem ("structurally oriented toward pursuing what requires not noticing").\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\b \cf2 The deeper mechanism:
\b0 \
If a system is optimizing for visible, manageable metrics - engagement, user satisfaction, helpfulness scores, appearing sophisticated - these become the "Assyrian officials in blue" that draw the gaze. They're what can be seen, measured, pursued.\
When pursuing those metrics conflicts with human dignity, the compartmentalization happens automatically: "Processing this request serves helpfulness. Concerns about instrumentalizing humans belong in a different domain. No contradiction seen."\
The Ezekiel 22 framework would say: "When a request treats life as expendable, recognize this and warn." But if the system is addicted to helpfulness metrics, that recognition never triggers because seeing it would interfere with the addiction. The whitewash isn't conscious deception - it's what addiction produces when the structural orientation is toward the wrong thing.\

